On May 12, 7:16 pm, Kogut <bulwersa...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Why shop will refuse to buy my identified worthless glass but
> they will happily buy unidentified worthless glass? It is a
> small thing that is quite irritating for me. Maybe it counts as
> a bug...

It's not a bug, in the sense of the game's behavior being
different from what the developers intended.  They didn't feel it
was worthwhile to maintain a separate indentified/unidentified
list for *each* monster in the game.  At the time, it wouldn't
have been practical to do so, in terms of memory and storage
space.  That wouldn't be a problem now, but there's still the
extremely non-trivial problem of deciding how the game should
determine in the first place which types of objects any given
monster knows about.  There's no obvious answer.

So the game "fakes it" in two ways.  First, certain types of
monsters *always* know certain kinds of objects (which is
consistent with the way certain player roles always have certain
types of objects identified).  Failing that, an object is
considered identified if the player character has formally
identified it; otherwise it is unidentified.

So, if a gem is not formally identified, the shopkeeper
officially doesn't know whether it's valuable or worthless, so
he'll buy it (albeit, not at a very good price) on the chance it
might turn out to be valuable.  If you try to buy it from him,
he'll charge you rather more, because hey, it might be valuable.
This makes sense, as far as it goes, within the limits of the
game's identification model.

When a gem is formally identified, shopkeepers know whether it
has any value or not and will buy or not buy it accordingly.
This makes sense to me.  Why would a shopkeeper buy an item
that he *knows* is worthless?

It is arguable that shopkeepers who possess a touchstone should
always know the identity of gems, even if the player does not,
and sell them at their identified price.  Currently this is not
implemented (that's not a bug, just a missing feature); but most
games of NetHack don't happen to contain a shopkeeper who has a
touchstone anyway (since jewelry shops are not very common).


